# check: fail error[O26]: this value refers to this function's own storage
# never: error[O10c]
# a function may return something built from a bare "&" parameter into its result scope: the parameter's
# scope is passed with it (O4b), and the relation becomes an obligation its callers discharge (O10b).
# This caller cannot - its argument is its own by-value parameter, and the result would outlive it: one error for the one
# cause (B11), the returned instance's binding (O13a) found before the obligation. (A local built here and passed on
# lives in the result scope instead, O26a.)
type P struct(x I32) { x }
type NB struct(inner P&) { inner }
fn wrap(p P&) NB& { return NB(p) }
fn bad(q P) NB& {
    return wrap(q)
}
test "never runs" { }
